Sync the docs with this round, and cut what the review found

Docs: testing.md/zh gets the real numbers (317 Rust tests, 37/37 evals, 39
app-server cases), a per-crate split that now mentions the hook event matrix,
the todo limits, the job ring and the goal loop, and a new section on what evals
cannot assert — multi-turn state, real processes, and anything a human has to
start — with the test that covers each instead. README gains the P9 roadmap row
and the background flag; AGENTS gains hooks/jobs/goals in the module map plus
three conventions this round kept re-learning: extensions are injected (tool
layer knows traits, app-server holds implementations), capabilities are switched
on by humans and the human-only paths have no tool at all, and nothing may be
silent.

ponytail-debt: zero `ponytail:` markers in the repo — this project tracks
deferrals in docs/limitations.md instead, and all six new ones are there in both
languages. Two of them named a ceiling but no trigger to revisit, which is how a
deferral rots: Windows hooks now say what first-class support would take, and the
in-process job table says to reach for `joy schedule` rather than persisting it.

ponytail-review: the cuts that survived are small. `find_ascii_ci` is now a
`windows().position()` instead of a hand-rolled index loop, and the job registry's
"no such job" answer is one function shared by the read and kill paths — two
copies of that string is how "someone else's job" leaks by wording alone. One
finding was rejected by its own test: the permission-mention flag in the
subagent report scanner looked like unused plumbing, but a test covers it and it
is deliberate, so it stayed. Net: -6 lines.
